6\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33731961\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35111752\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4047044 \"An enteric glial cell that has an elongated, fibrous morphology with long processes that run parallel to nerve fibers connecting enteric myenteric ganglia. This cell is located in the interganglionic fiber tracts of the enteric nervous system.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:contributor obo:CL_4047044 <https://orcid.org/0009-0005-7919-4905>)\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:date obo:CL_4047044 \"2025-02-11T12:28:37Z\"^^xsd:dateTime)\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35111752\") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4047044 \"type II enteric glia\")\nAnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4047044 \"type 2 interganglionic glia\")\nSubClassOf(obo:CL_4047044 obo:CL_4040002)\nSubClassOf(obo:CL_4047044 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000050 obo:UBERON_0000429))\n\n# Class: obo:CL_4047045 (type 3 mucosal glia)\n\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:25161129\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33731961\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:37153631\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:38957473\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4047045 \"An enteric glial cell located in the lamina propria of the intestinal mucosa. This cell plays important roles in maintaining gut barrier function, regulating intestinal homeostasis, and mediating interactions between the enteric nervous system and the immune system.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:contributor obo:CL_4047045 <https://orcid.org/0009-0005-7919-4905>)\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:date obo:CL_4047045 \"2025-02-11T12:39:11Z\"^^xsd:dateTime)\nAnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4047045 \"type 3 mucosal glia\")\nSubClassOf(obo:CL_4047045 obo:CL_4040002)\nSubClassOf(obo:CL_4047045 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000050 obo:UBERON_0000030))\n\n# Class: obo:CL_4047047 (type I enteric glial cell)\n\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"DOI:10.1016/j.celrep.2023.112194\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:29927320\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33731961\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35111752\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4047047 \"An enteric glial cell located within the ganglia of the enteric nervous system. This cell is characterized by it’s small somata and short, irregularly branched processes that surround neuronal cell bodies in the myenteric and submucosal ganglia. This cell plays important roles in modulating neuronal activity and neurogenesis in the gastrointestinal tract.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:contributor obo:CL_4047047 <https://orcid.org/0009-0005-7919-4905>)\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:date obo:CL_4047047 \"2025-02-11T13:35:14Z\"^^xsd:dateTime)\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33731961\") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4047047 \"intraganglionic glia\")\nAnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4047047 \"type 1 enteric glia\")\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35111752\") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4047047 \"type I enteric glia\")\nAnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4047047 \"type I enteric glial cell\")\nSubClassOf(obo:CL_4047047 obo:CL_4040002)\nSubClassOf(obo:CL_4047047 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000050 obo:UBERON_0001809))\n\n# Class: obo:CL_4047048 (type 4 muscularis propria glia)\n\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33731961\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:34401661\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4047048 \"An enteric glial cell characterized by a small central soma that extends long, thin, bipolar projections. This cell is primarily located along small nerve fibers within the muscle layers of the gastrointestinal tract and is known for its unbranching processes that closely follow these nerve fibers in the muscularis.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:contributor obo:CL_4047048 <https://orcid.org/0009-0005-7919-4905>)\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:date obo:CL_4047048 \"2025-02-18T11:14:25Z\"^^xsd:dateTime)\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:34401661\") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_4047048 \"intramuscular glia\")\nAnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4047048 \"type 4 muscularis propria glia\")\nSubClassOf(obo:CL_4047048 obo:CL_4040002)\nSubClassOf(obo:CL_4047048 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000050 obo:UBERON_0006660))\n\n# Class: obo:CL_4047051 (small intestine BEST4+ enterocyte)\n\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:24223998\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:34497389\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4047051 \"An enterocyte of the human small intestine expressing bestrophin-4 (BEST4) calcium-activated ion channels.  These enterocytes have a disinctive transcriptomic profile and are scattered through the small intestine epithelium.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:contributor obo:CL_4047051 <https://orcid.org/0009-0005-7919-4905>)\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:date obo:CL_4047051 \"2024-07-25T13:05:50Z\"^^xsd:dateTime)\nAnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4047051 \"small intestine BEST4+ enterocyte\")\nEquivalentClasses(obo:CL_4047051 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:CL_0000584 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000050 obo:UBERON_0001902) ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002292 obo:PR_Q8NFU0)))\nSubClassOf(obo:CL_4047051 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000050 obo:UBERON_0001902))\n\n# Class: obo:CL_4047052 (BEST4+ colonocyte)\n\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:24223998\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:34497389\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4047052 \"An enterocyte of the human colon expressing bestrophin-4 (BEST4) calcium-activated ion channels.  These cells have a distinct transcriptomic profile compared to other enterocytes and are scattered through the colon epithelium.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:contributor obo:CL_4047052 <https://orcid.org/0009-0005-7919-4905>)\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:date obo:CL_4047052 \"2024-07-25T13:21:19Z\"^^xsd:dateTime)\nAnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4047052 \"BEST4+ colonocyte\")\nEquivalentClasses(obo:CL_4047052 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:CL_1000347 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002292 obo:PR_Q8NFU0)))\n\n# Class: obo:CL_4047053 (TREM2-positive macrophage)\n\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33033253\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33628208\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35746551\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:36750615\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:39369473\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4047053 \"A macrophage characterized by high expression of Triggering Receptor Expressed on Myeloid cells 2 (TREM2), found in various tissues including the liver, adipose tissue, bone, gut (Colonna, 2023), and tumor microenvironments, where it is associated with immunosuppressive and anti-inflammatory activity (Colmenares, 2024; Khantakova, 2022). This cell exhibits a distinct gene expression profile in the tumor microenvironment, including overexpression of complement system genes (C1QA, C1QB, C1QC, C3), and SPP1 in both mice and humans (Xiong, 2020; Khantakova, 2022). It is involved in phagocytosis, tissue repair, and modulation of immune responses (Coeho, 2021).\")\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:contributor obo:CL_4047053 <https://orcid.org/0009-0005-7919-4905>)\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:date obo:CL_4047053 \"2025-03-17T17:20:08Z\"^^xsd:dateTime)\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:39369473\") oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym obo:CL_4047053 \"TREM-2 + resident macrophage\")\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35746551\") oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym obo:CL_4047053 \"TREM2+ macrophage\")\nAnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4047053 \"TREM2-positive macrophage\")\nEquivalentClasses(obo:CL_4047053 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:CL_0000235 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002292 obo:PR_000016626)))\n\n# Class: obo:CL_4047054 (migratory dendritic cell)\n\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:28845904\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33681216\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:34302064\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4047054 \"A dendritic cell capable of capturing antigens in peripheral tissues and migrating through blood or lymphatic vessels to secondary lymphoid organs, where it presents processed antigens to T cells. It expresses MHC class II molecules and lacks lineage markers associated with other leukocyte populations, distinguishing it as a member of the dendritic cell lineage. This cell plays a pivotal role in initiating and regulating adaptive immune responses by linking peripheral antigen capture with T cell activation in lymphoid tissues.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:contributor obo:CL_4047054 <https://orcid.org/0009-0005-7919-4905>)\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:date obo:CL_4047054 \"2025-03-17T17:37:37Z\"^^xsd:dateTime)\nAnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4047054 \"migratory dendritic cell\")\nSubClassOf(obo:CL_4047054 obo:CL_0000451)\nSubClassOf(obo:CL_4047054 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002215 obo:GO_0002407))\nSubClassOf(obo:CL_4047054 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002215 obo:GO_0002468))\n\n# Class: obo:CL_4047056 (large mucus secreting cholangiocyte)\n\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:15579435\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:26273695\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:28261592\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"doi:10.1002/hep.25595\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"doi:10.1152/ajpgi.00227.2012\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4047056 \"A columnar, mucin-producing cholangiocyte that lines large bile ducts, including the hilar bile duct, right and left hepatic bile ducts, and large intrahepatic bile ducts. This cell expresses phosphorylated STAT3 and TFF-3 in humans and mice.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:contributor obo:CL_4047056 <https://orcid.org/0009-0005-7919-4905>)\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:date obo:CL_4047056 \"2025-03-20T14:21:41Z\"^^xsd:dateTime)\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"doi:10.1152/ajpgi.00227.2012\") oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym obo:CL_4047056 \"mucin-secreting cholangiocyte\")\nAnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4047056 \"large mucus secreting cholangiocyte\")\nSubClassOf(obo:CL_4047056 obo:CL_1000488)\nSubClassOf(obo:CL_4047056 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002215 obo:GO_0070254))\n\n# Class: obo:CL_4047060 (BAG3-positive monocyte)\n\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:20018251\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"doi:10.21203/rs.3.rs-3229780/v1\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4047060 \"A circulating mononuclear phagocyte characterized by the expression of BCL-2-associated athanogene 3 (BAG3) protein, a co-chaperone involved in protein quality control and anti-apoptotic processes. (Fontanella, 2009). It undergoes cellular stress during the differentiation into M2 macrophages and is linked to poor survival rates in multiple cancer types. (Park, 2023)\")\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:contributor obo:CL_4047060 <https://orcid.org/0009-0005-7919-4905>)\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:date obo:CL_4047060 \"2025-05-06T14:41:24Z\"^^xsd:dateTime)\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"doi:10.21203/rs.3.rs-3229780/v1\") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4047060 \"BAG3+ monocyte\")\nAnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4047060 \"BAG3-positive monocyte\")\nEquivalentClasses(obo:CL_4047060 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:CL_0000576 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002292 obo:PR_000004621)))\n\n# Class: obo:CL_4047101 (liver-resident natural killer cell)\n\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:26330348\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:26639736\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:27798170\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31114585\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:32351704\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:34230995\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35865550\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4047101 \"A natural killer cell resident to the liver, located in the hepatic sinusoids. In humans this cell type is distinguished from circulating natural killer cells by CD49a or CD69 gene expression. Liver-resident natural killer cells have also been shown to express CCR5, EOMES, KLRB1, GZMK, and CXCR6 in humans.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:contributor obo:CL_4047101 <https://orcid.org/0009-0005-7919-4905>)\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:contributor obo:CL_4047101 \"https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8134-3037\")\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:date obo:CL_4047101 \"2024-07-29T13:43:11Z\"^^xsd:dateTime)\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31114585\") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4047101 \"he-NK\")\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"doi:10.3389/fimmu.2019.00946\") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4047101 \"hepatic NK\")\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"doi:10.3389/fimmu.2019.00946\") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4047101 \"hepatic natural killer cells\")\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33828559\") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4047101 \"intrahepatic NK\")\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:26639736\") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4047101 \"liver NK cell\")\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:26330348\") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4047101 \"liver resident natural killer cell\")\nAnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4047101 \"liver-specific natural killer cell\")\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35726345\") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4047101 \"lr-NK\")\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:34230995\") rdfs:comment obo:CL_4047101 \"Liver-resident natural killer cells play a crucial role in modulating the immune response during inflammation by balancing cytotoxic activity and cytokine production, thereby contributing to the maintenance of liver homeostasis and protection against excessive tissue damage.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4047101 \"liver-resident natural killer cell\")\nEquivalentClasses(obo:CL_4047101 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:CL_0000623 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0001025 obo:UBERON_0001281)))\n\n# Class: obo:CL_4052001 (multiciliated ependymal cell)\n\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:25045600\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:28067220\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:37008045\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052001 \"An ependymal cell that lines the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles of the brain. The cell is characterized by multiple motile cilia on its apical surface, which beats in a coordinated manner to facilitate the movement of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), contributing to brain homeostasis.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:contributor obo:CL_4052001 <https://orcid.org/0009-0000-8480-9277>)\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:date obo:CL_4052001 \"2024-08-20T10:06:15Z\"^^xsd:dateTime)\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:25045600\") oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym obo:CL_4052001 \"multiciliated ependymal cells\")\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:28067220\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:37008045\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType obo:OMO_0003000) oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_4052001 \"E1\")\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:25045600\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:27311928\") rdfs:comment obo:CL_4052001 \"multiciliated ependymal cell, with an average of 16 motile cilia per cell, exhibits two types of planar cell polarity (PCP): rotational polarity, with unidirectional cilia orientation, and translational polarity, with asymmetric cilia positioning in the apical area. This location-specific PCP varies across the ventricular wall, aligning with the direction of CSF flow. These cells are interconnected by adherens and gap junctions, which support intercellular communication and maintain the structural integrity of the ependymal layer. Unlike some other cell types, these cells lack tight junctions.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4052001 \"multiciliated ependymal cell\")\nEquivalentClasses(obo:CL_4052001 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:CL_0000075 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000050 obo:UBERON_0004670) ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000051 obo:GO_0031514) ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0000053 obo:PATO_0010007)))\n\n# Class: obo:CL_4052002 (syncytial cell)\n\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:20851884\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"Wikipedia:Syncytium\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052002 \"A multinucleate cell formed by the fusion of multiple uninuclear cells through plasma membrane fusion. This process leads to a single large cell containing multiple nuclei within a shared cytoplasm.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:contributor obo:CL_4052002 <https://orcid.org/0009-0000-8480-9277>)\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:date obo:CL_4052002 \"2024-08-15T10:38:46Z\"^^xsd:dateTime)\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"WBbt:0008074\") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4052002 \"syncytium\")\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:9067520\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasSynonymType obo:OMO_0003000) oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_4052002 \"SC\")\nAnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4052002 \"syncytial cell\")\nEquivalentClasses(obo:CL_4052002 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:CL_0000000 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0000053 obo:PATO_0001908) ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0002353 obo:GO_0000768)))\n\n# Class: obo:CL_4052003 (intestinal villus capillary endothelial cell)\n\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:18480313\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35810168\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:38051275\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052003 \"A capillary endothelial cell that is part of the intestinal villus. This cell is highly fenestrated, with fenestrations most numerous at the villus tips, and plays a vital role in nutrient absorption and maintaining the selective permeability of the intestinal barrier.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:contributor obo:CL_4052003 <https://orcid.org/0009-0000-8480-9277>)\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:date obo:CL_4052003 \"2024-09-17T10:03:25Z\"^^xsd:dateTime)\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35810168\") oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym obo:CL_4052003 \"villus blood capillary endothelial cells\")\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:38051275\") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_4052003 \"villus tip endothelial cell\")\nAnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4052003 \"intestinal villus capillary endothelial cell\")\nEquivalentClasses(obo:CL_4052003 ObjectIntersectionOf(obo:CL_0002144 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000050 obo:UBERON_0001213)))\nSubClassOf(obo:CL_4052003 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0000053 obo:PATO_0002064))\n\n# Class: obo:CL_4052004 (intestinal fibroblastic reticular cell)\n\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33707780\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35440118\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052004 \"A specialized fibroblastic reticular cell that is part of gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), responsible for forming a structural network that facilitates immune cell interactions. This cell is found in Peyer's patches, cryptopatches, and isolated lymphoid follicles. It plays crucial roles in maintaining intestinal immunity by controlling innate lymphoid cell homeostasis and function, organizing lymphoid structures, and contributing to intestinal microbiome balance.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:contributor obo:CL_4052004 <https://orcid.org/0009-0000-8480-9277>)\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:date obo:CL_4052004 \"2024-09-19T11:22:18Z\"^^xsd:dateTime)\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33707780\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35440118\") terms:description obo:CL_4052004 \"Intestinal fibroblastic reticular cells develop from both perivascular and subepithelial progenitors during Peyer's patch formation. Intestinal fibroblastic reticular cells express key markers such as CCL19, CCL21, CXCL13, TNFSF13B (also known as BAFF), and produce IL-7, which are important for their immune regulatory functions and the organization of lymphoid structures.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35440118\") oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym obo:CL_4052004 \"Ccl19-expressing fibroblastic reticular cell\")\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35440118\") oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym obo:CL_4052004 \"solitary intestinal lymphoid tissue fibroblastic reticular cell\")\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35440118\") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_4052004 \"gut-associated secondary lymphoid organs fibroblastic reticular cell\")\nAnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4052004 \"intestinal fibroblastic reticular cell\")\nSubClassOf(obo:CL_4052004 obo:CL_0009101)\nSubClassOf(obo:CL_4052004 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000050 obo:UBERON_0001962))\n\n# Class: obo:CL_4052005 (intestinal subserosal fibroblast)\n\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:23143399\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:26694715\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:38877292\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052005 \"A fibroblast that is part of the subserosa of intestine. This cell interacts with immune cells and play roles in inflammation and fibrosis. In certain conditions, such as Crohn's disease, subserosal fibroblast may differentiate into myofibroblasts.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:contributor obo:CL_4052005 <https://orcid.org/0009-0000-8480-9277>)\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:date obo:CL_4052005 \"2024-09-19T12:58:29Z\"^^xsd:dateTime)\nAnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4052005 \"intestinal subserosal fibroblast\")\nSubClassOf(obo:CL_4052005 obo:CL_0000057)\nSubClassOf(obo:CL_4052005 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000050 obo:UBERON_0001243))\nSubClassOf(obo:CL_4052005 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000050 obo:UBERON_0012375))\n\n# Class: obo:CL_4052006 (intestinal enteroendocrine progenitor)\n\nAnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31756561\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:32531023\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35913117\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:38260422\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"doi:/10.1038/s41467-021-27901-5\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052006 \"An epithelial cell that is part of the crypt of Lieberkuhn, originating from intestinal stem cells and giving rise to enteroendocrine cells (EECs). In mouse and human, this cell can be characterized by the expression of Neurog3, and has the ability to proliferate and differentiate into multiple EEC subtypes. Its proliferative potential contributes to crypt growth, distinguishing it from fully differentiated EECs.\")\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:contributor obo:CL_4052006 <https://orcid.org/0009-0000-8480-9277>)\nAnnotationAssertion(terms:date obo:CL_4052006 \"2024-09-20T12:13:26Z\"^^xsd:dateTime)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
